THIRD-PARTY NOTICES AND ATTRIBUTION

`fast-n-rust-everything` (`fast-n-rust` / `fnr`) builds directly upon two MIT-licensed
open-source projects and combines their engines into a unified CLI and MCP server:

1. rust-anything (v3.3.0)
   Repository: https://github.com/erkamyaman/rust-anything
   Author: Erkam Yaman (erkamyaman)
   License: MIT
   Incorporated into: `crates/migrate-engine/` (AST analysis, multi-language bridges,
   Rayon batching, semantic-trap recording, diff, fuzz, shadow, gate, packaging,
   whole-app front-door / wire / lift).

2. fast-anything (v0.8.5)
   Repository: https://github.com/EL4CTEO/fast-anything
   Author: EL4CTEO
   License: MIT
   Incorporated into: `crates/fast-engine/` and `examples/` (hotspot profiling across
   Python, JS/TS/Bun/Deno, Ruby, Java, Kotlin, Go, PHP, C, C++, C#, R; up to 500
   real call recording + shrink/edge-case synthesis; fast one-function inline swap
   and interleaved app benchmarking).

What `fast-n-rust-everything` adds on top (`crates/cli/` and engine upgrades):
- Unified `fast-n-rust` and `fnr` binaries + 29-tool threaded MCP server with 4 prompts
- Cross-engine synergies (`synergy.rs`):
  * Mode Advisor (`fast` 5% vs `--batch` Rayon vs `whole-app`)
  * `TASK.md` enrichment with static risk detection & `crates.toml` suggestions
  * `fnr escalate <id>`: promote a `.fast/ports/<id>` port into a `rust/` unit crate
  * `fnr verify --gate` and `fnr go --strict` running `fmt`, `clippy -D warnings`,
    `cargo test`, and `unsafe` SAFETY-comment checks on hotspot ports
  * Auto-detection of `.fast` hotspot ports vs `rust/` unit crates in `gate`, `enable`,
    `disable`, `bench`, and `report`
- Unified `doctor` (all 14 languages, AI coding agents, cache disk usage)
- Unified `setup` (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Antigravity, OpenCode)
- Safe `clean` command (`--cache`, `--ports`, `--rust`, `--all`, `--dry-run`)
- Dual kill-switch compatibility (`FNR_DISABLE=1` alongside `FAST_ANYTHING_DISABLE=1`
  and `RUST_ANYTHING_MODE=old`)
- Fixes for upstream issues (rust-anything #288, #289, #290, #291, #300, #301 and
  fast-anything #76).
